## Abstract

Polymers, most often epoxies, filled with hexagonal boron nitride (h-BN), BN nanosheets (BNNS) or aluminium nitride (AlN) are used as thermal interface and encapsulation materials in semiconductor packages because they conduct heat while remaining electrical insulators. Their thermal conductivity *k* is reported in hundreds of papers, but it is measured with laser flash, transient plane-source, hot-bridge, hot-wire and steady-state methods that do not sense the same quantity. We built a source-verified dataset of **{{ n_rows }} measurements from {{ n_papers }} open-access papers** ({{ stats.year_min }}–{{ stats.year_max }}), in which every value is traceable to a table, a sentence or a pixel-digitized figure, and used it to answer three characterization questions.

**How much does the method shift *k*?** Samples reported in both directions have a median in-plane/through-plane ratio of {{ an.A_median|f2 }}. An isotropic-mode transient sensor would therefore read up to {{ an.implied_isotropic_sensor_over_through_plane.at_A_max|f2 }}× the laser-flash through-plane value on such a sample. Between papers, isotropic-mode sensors report {{ eff["method:MIXED"].factor|f2 }}× the *k* of laser flash at equal loading (95% CI {{ eff["method:MIXED"].ci95[0]|f2 }}–{{ eff["method:MIXED"].ci95[1]|f2 }}). Paper-to-paper scatter (×{{ main.between_paper_factor_1sd|f2 }} at one standard deviation) far exceeds instrument uncertainty (about {{ budget.median_rel_sigma_all|pct }}).

**Do effective-medium models explain the data?** {{ phys.maxwell_ceiling.share|pct }} of rows exceed the ceiling of any isolated-sphere (Maxwell) model, and the Nan 1997 platelet model needs an apparent interfacial resistance of {{ phys.parameters.nan_R_K_m2K_W.median|sci }} m²K/W (median), inside the range of published fits.

**Can *k* be predicted for a paper that was never seen?** With validation that holds out whole papers, {{ best_phrase }} reaches a typical error of ×{{ M[best].lopo.mae_factor|f2 }}, against {% if bbase == "physics" %}×{{ M.physics.lopo.mae_factor|f2 }} for calibrated physics, the best baseline.{% else %}×{{ M[bbase].lopo.mae_factor|f2 }} for the best baseline and ×{{ M.physics.lopo.mae_factor|f2 }} for calibrated physics.{% endif %} Splitting by row instead overstates accuracy.{% if ext %} Tested on {{ ext.test.rows }} measurements from {{ ext.test.papers }} papers curated by others, the best model trained here ({{ ext.top.label }}) reaches ×{{ ext.top.factor|f2 }} against ×{{ ext.phys.factor|f2 }} for calibrated physics{% if ext.top.vs and ext.top.vs["clear"] %}, a clear improvement{% else %}, a gap still within the uncertainty of {{ ext.test.papers }} test papers{% endif %}. Our effective-medium code reproduces {{ ext.code.n_exact }} of {{ ext.code.n_models }} published model columns of two independent implementations exactly (where the last one differs, the published values fail the model's own equation).{% endif %}


