### 4.3 What physical models can and cannot explain

![k/k_m against loading with model curves]({{ figs.loading }})

*Figure 3. Enhancement* k/k<sub>m</sub> *against loading, by filler family, with the Maxwell ceiling (filler* k *→ ∞) and Nan 1997 for randomly oriented fillers, with perfect interfaces and with the median fitted R<sub>K</sub>. Model curves assume k<sub>m</sub> = 0.2 W/m·K, 10 µm fillers and t/D = 0.05 (h-BN), 0.01 (BNNS) or spheres (AlN). For AlN spheres the perfect-interface curve lies on the Maxwell ceiling, because the filler is about 1,400 times more conductive than the matrix.*

**The sphere ceiling.** For isolated spheres, even infinitely conductive ones, Maxwell–Garnett cannot exceed *k*/*k<sub>m</sub>* = (1 + 2*φ*)/(1 − *φ*). **{{ phys.maxwell_ceiling.above_maxwell_ceiling }} of {{ phys.maxwell_ceiling.rows_with_phi }} rows with a known loading ({{ phys.maxwell_ceiling.share|pct }}) lie above that ceiling**. The Maxwell fit hit its parameter bound in all {{ phys.series }} loading series. Plate-like fillers, contact networks and alignment are therefore essential to these composites, not refinements.

**Platelets with interfacial resistance.** The Nan model for random h-BN platelets brackets most data between its perfect-interface and finite-*R<sub>K</sub>* curves. Fitting one *R<sub>K</sub>* per series gives a median of {{ phys.parameters.nan_R_K_m2K_W.median|sci }} m²K/W (interquartile range {{ phys.parameters.nan_R_K_m2K_W.iqr[0]|sci }}–{{ phys.parameters.nan_R_K_m2K_W.iqr[1]|sci }}; semi-axis convention). That is inside the 10⁻⁸–2.4×10⁻⁶ range of published BN/epoxy fits [14, 15], and two to three orders of magnitude above molecular-dynamics values for ideal interfaces. The fitted resistance is therefore an *apparent* quantity that also absorbs voids, poor dispersion and filler–filler contacts. In {{ phys.parameters.nan_R_K_m2K_W.series_above_perfect_interface }} of {{ phys.series }} series the data exceed even the perfect-interface prediction, which points to alignment or percolating networks. Lewis–Nielsen, with *k<sub>f</sub>* fixed at the intrinsic value, fits with a median shape factor *A* = {{ phys.parameters.lewis_nielsen_A.median }}; for comparison, an earlier study fitted *A* = 31 with an apparent *k<sub>f</sub>* of 16 W/m·K [16].

