## 5. Discussion

### 5.1 Implications for characterization practice

The analyses point to a practical conclusion for anyone measuring or citing *k* of composite TIMs. **Without a direction, *k* of a platelet composite is not a material property; it is a property of the measurement.** A factor of {{ eff.in_plane.factor|round(1) }} separates in-plane from through-plane values in the mixed model ({{ an.A_median|round(1) }} at the median for samples measured both ways), and up to √*A* separates an isotropic transient reading from a laser-flash disc on such a sample. Both are larger than any instrument's stated uncertainty.

For package design, the through-plane value is what matters, and only a multi-thickness D5470 measurement captures the contact resistance that dominates thin bond lines [12]. A literature laser-flash *k* is best read as an upper bound on in-package performance unless the bond line is thick and the contacts are good.

Certified references bracket this range well and give a cheap way to check calibration:
- Pyroceram 9606 (BCR-724), about 4 W/m·K;
- Pyrex 7740 (BCR-039), about 1.1 W/m·K [18].

### 5.2 What the papers left out, and a reporting checklist

| Information not stated in the source | Rows missing it | Share |
|---|---|---|
{% for g in gaps %}
| {{ g.item }} | {{ g.missing }} of {{ g.of }} | {{ g.share|pct }} |
{% endfor %}

These gaps limit every analysis above. We therefore suggest a minimum report for any *k* measurement on a filled polymer:
1. **Direction** (in-plane or through-plane) and sample geometry: thickness and lateral size.
2. **Technique and mode:** for Hot Disk, isotropic or anisotropic module and sensor radius; for laser flash, the holder (standard or in-plane) and the data-reduction model; for D5470, the pressure and the number of thicknesses.
3. **Sources of *c<sub>p</sub>* and *ρ*** for diffusivity methods: DSC or literature, and measured or rule-of-mixtures density.
4. **Neat-matrix *k*** measured with the same instrument and protocol.
5. **Loading in both vol% and wt%**, stated as a fraction of the whole composite including hardener.
6. **Filler size, aspect ratio and orientation** (or an orientation measure).
7. **Temperature, repeats and uncertainty**, plus a reference material if one was used.

### 5.3 Limitations

- **Open-access scope.** The dataset is limited to open-access full texts and, for a few rows, publicly available abstracts. About 200 publisher-blocked papers were logged but not used, which may bias the sample toward certain journals and groups.
{% if acq.figure_only %}
- **Figure-only values not yet digitized.** The {{ acq.papers }} papers added in v1.2 also show {{ acq.figure_only }} values only in figures. They were counted but not digitized, so those papers are represented by what they print in text and tables.
{% endif %}
- **Lower-confidence rows.** {{ stats.confidence.Medium }} rows are Medium confidence (abstract-only, digitized, or printed as approximate or as a bound). Abstract-only values appear biased upward, since abstracts quote a paper's best result.
- **Between-paper method effects are associations.** Methods are confounded with everything else that differs between papers. The decisive experiment, one set of samples measured by several techniques, is not in the literature we could access.
{% if ext %}
- **External tests are small in papers.** The external test set has {{ ext.test.papers }} papers, so differences between models of a few hundredths in log₁₀ are within its paper-level uncertainty; only the differences marked *clear* in Section 4.5 are.
{% endif %}
- **Physical-model inputs.** Filler geometry and intrinsic properties are assumed where not reported, so fitted *R<sub>K</sub>* values are diagnostics rather than interface physics.

## 6. Conclusions

- A source-verified, characterization-aware dataset of {{ n_rows }} measurements shows that **how *k* was measured matters as much as what was measured**:
  - in-plane and through-plane values of the same sample differ by a median factor of {{ an.A_median|f2 }};
  - an isotropic transient sensor would differ from a laser-flash disc by up to ×{{ an.implied_isotropic_sensor_over_through_plane.at_A_max|f2 }};
  - paper-to-paper scatter (×{{ main.between_paper_factor_1sd|f2 }} at 1 SD) dwarfs instrument uncertainty.
- {{ phys.maxwell_ceiling.share|pct }} of measurements exceed the isolated-sphere ceiling. Plate-like fillers and contacts are essential. The apparent interfacial resistances needed to explain the data ({{ phys.parameters.nan_R_K_m2K_W.median|sci }} m²K/W median) match published fits, not ideal-interface simulations.
- Holding out whole papers, {{ best_phrase }} predicts *k* to a typical ×{{ M[best].lopo.mae_factor|f2 }}. Random row splits would have overstated this.{% if abls.meta_top %} The most useful metadata after composition is {{ abls.meta_top }}.{% endif +%}
{% if ext %}
- On literature curated by others ({{ ext.test.rows }} measurements, {{ ext.test.papers }} papers), the {{ ext.top.label }} predicts *k* to ×{{ ext.top.factor|f2 }} against ×{{ ext.phys.factor|f2 }} for calibrated physics{% if ext.top.vs and ext.top.vs["clear"] %}, clearly better{% else %}; with {{ ext.test.papers }} test papers that gap is not yet distinguishable from zero{% endif %}, and its 90% intervals cover {{ ext.top.coverage|pct }} of rows. {% if ext.clearly_worse %}Unconstrained trees ({{ ext.clearly_worse|join(", ") }}) are clearly worse than physics alone. {% endif %}Publication year, a proxy for paper identity, had to be dropped as a feature for predictions to transfer.
{% endif %}
- The single most valuable addition to this literature would be the same samples measured by laser flash, Hot Disk (both modes) and multi-thickness D5470, reported with the checklist in Section 5.2.

